@startuml INA700_pi_threads
' INA700 - the Pi's threads and what passes between them
' Author: Suraj Karki
' Course: INA700 - AI-based pick and place
'
' The course asks for multithreading where each thread owns one distinct
' responsibility. These are those threads. Nothing is shared between them
' except bounded queues and one guarded snapshot, so there is no lock to
' forget and no state two threads can disagree about.
'
' Every queue holds at most a frame or two on purpose. The camera produces
' fifteen frames a second and inference manages two or three; put_newest
' therefore DROPS the stale frame rather than queueing it, because a queue
' that fills is a system showing the operator a picture of the past.

title INA700 - threads, queues and the shared snapshot

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 18
skinparam ranksep 34
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #333333
  BackgroundColor #EFF6EC
  BackgroundColor<<queue>> #EAF2FB
  BackgroundColor<<outside>> #FBEAEA
  BackgroundColor<<state>> #F2F2EE
}

rectangle "Basler camera" as CAM <<outside>>
rectangle "ABB controller\n(RAPID client)" as ROBOT <<outside>>
rectangle "operator" as HUMAN <<outside>>

rectangle "1  CaptureWorker\n--\ngrabs frames at capture_fps\npublishes the image for the preview" as T1
rectangle "2  InferenceWorker\n--\nVisionPipeline.process\ndetect, orient, colour, calibrate\nprints the inference time" as T2
rectangle "3  MappingWorker\n--\nCoordinateService\npixels to millimetres, and rz" as T3
rectangle "4  CycleWorker\n--\nCycleController\nselect, command, verify, retry" as T4
rectangle "5  ConnectionWorker\n--\naccepts the robot's connection\nowns connected / not connected" as T5
rectangle "main thread\n--\nTkView or ConsoleView\nrenders the snapshot" as MAIN

rectangle "frames\nmaxsize 2" as Q1 <<queue>>
rectangle "results\nmaxsize 2\n(pixels)" as Q2 <<queue>>
rectangle "scenes\nmaxsize 2\n(millimetres)" as Q3 <<queue>>
rectangle "requests\nmaxsize 8\n(PickRequest)" as Q4 <<queue>>
rectangle "SharedState\n--\none immutable StateSnapshot\nbehind one lock" as STATE <<state>>

CAM --> T1 : pypylon grab
T1 --> Q1 : put_newest(Frame)
Q1 --> T2
T2 --> Q2 : put_newest(InferenceResult)
Q2 --> T3
T3 --> Q3 : put_newest(MappedScene)
Q3 --> T4 : LatestResultHolder\ntakes the freshest, not the oldest

HUMAN --> MAIN : shape and colour,\nSend pick to robot
HUMAN ..> Q4 : typed line, or the pipe
MAIN --> Q4 : PickRequest
Q4 --> T4

T4 --> ROBOT : move <shape> x y rz#
ROBOT --> T4 : DONE: / ERROR:
T5 --> ROBOT : listens on 0.0.0.0:5000

T1 ..> STATE : the newest image
T2 ..> STATE : boxes, inference time
T3 ..> STATE : poses in millimetres
T4 ..> STATE : status, cycle report
T5 ..> STATE : connected
STATE ..> MAIN : snapshot(), ten times a second

note right of Q1
  put_newest, not put. When inference is
  busy the stale frame is thrown away and
  the new one takes its place: the robot
  must be told where the object is NOW,
  and a queue that fills would hand it a
  position from several seconds ago.
end note

note right of MAIN
  The view owns the main thread because Tk
  insists on it. That is why the terminal
  prompt is a command SOURCE on a thread of
  its own rather than a second view - it is
  what lets the window and the terminal both
  be live at once.
end note

note bottom of T4
  Nothing moves when the command is sent.
  The robot holds the position until the
  yellow button is pressed at the cell;
  the status line says which of the two
  buttons the cell is waiting for.
end note

@enduml
